From: Caboose <caboose@local>
Subject: [PATCH 93/93] Fill invalidated coherent memory on first use instead
 of reading it back at once

After a wait the invalidate read back every GPU-written range of host-coherent memory that
no completion record delivered, one round trip per 64 KiB, whether or not the application
would read it. A D3D11 game's staging readbacks and a GL driver's mapped buffers often
read only part of what the GPU wrote, or nothing.

vkInvalidateMappedMemoryRanges now leaves the blocks of watched coherent memory that can
wait lazy (caboose-bounded-memory/write_watch.h): their pages are dropped, and the first
access to one, by the application or the kernel, is filled from the phone by the write
watch's filler, three 16 KiB blocks to a round trip, write-protected so later writes are
recorded as before. Blocks holding writes not yet sent, outside the mapping, or of memory a
completion record is being stored into are read at once, as before. The decision is made
with the encoder held; a flush fills the lazy blocks of what it sends first, an unmap fills
the memory's lazy blocks while it is still mapped, and the flush shadow forgets a lazy
block's bytes. CABOOSE_LAZY_READBACK=0 reads everything at once.
